import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Graded Modal Sanada Arrow Calculus","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/explanation/graded-modal-sanada-arrow-calculus.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/explanation/graded-modal-sanada-arrow-calculus.md"}`),a={name:`handl/explanation/graded-modal-sanada-arrow-calculus.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="graded-modal-sanada-arrow-calculus" tabindex="-1">Graded Modal Sanada Arrow Calculus <a class="header-anchor" href="#graded-modal-sanada-arrow-calculus" aria-label="Permalink to “Graded Modal Sanada Arrow Calculus”">​</a></h1><p>Handl&#39;s canonical execution calculus is the <strong>Graded Modal Sanada Arrow Calculus (GMSAC)</strong>. It layers three ideas on top of one another:</p><ol><li><strong>Arrows</strong> — Hughes&#39; arrow calculus, with <code>arr</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>first</code>, <code>choice</code>, <code>loop</code>, and <code>app</code>.</li><li><strong>Grades</strong> — a row of algebraic capabilities that annotate an arrow type, such as <code>~[(Op, Query)]~&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Sanada&#39;s handlers</strong> — an operation request with an explicit continuation, and a scoped interpretation of those requests.</li></ol><p>This layering keeps the kernel tiny and generic while allowing an open-ended set of effect algebras to be declared in ordinary packages.</p><h2 id="the-three-layers" tabindex="-1">The three layers <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-three-layers" aria-label="Permalink to “The three layers”">​</a></h2><h3 id="arrows-hughes" tabindex="-1">Arrows (Hughes) <a class="header-anchor" href="#arrows-hughes" aria-label="Permalink to “Arrows (Hughes)”">​</a></h3><p>The base is a standard arrow calculus. Arrows are closed under sequential composition, firsting, choice, looping, and application. These combinators are all pure; they build computations but do not themselves interpret any effect.</p><h3 id="grades-modal-capabilities" tabindex="-1">Grades (modal capabilities) <a class="header-anchor" href="#grades-modal-capabilities" aria-label="Permalink to “Grades (modal capabilities)”">​</a></h3><p>A grade row is a multiset of grade terms attached to an arrow type:</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>f : A ~[(Op, Query)]~&gt; B</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>Op</code> is the marker grade that says &quot;this arrow is an operation request&quot;. <code>Query</code> is the algebra grade — the arrow is interpreted by the active <code>Query</code> handler. Multiple grades combine in one row:</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>g : A ~[(Op, Clock, FFI)]~&gt; B</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Here <code>Clock</code> and <code>FFI</code> are two algebra grades the operation carries. The innermost handler matching any of them may intercept the operation.</p><p>The grade row is a modality. It does not just describe side effects; it states in which contexts the arrow may be interpreted. The <code>Op</code> marker turns a pure arrow type into a modal type.</p><h3 id="sanada-handlers" tabindex="-1">Sanada handlers <a class="header-anchor" href="#sanada-handlers" aria-label="Permalink to “Sanada handlers”">​</a></h3><p><code>Term::Op</code> and <code>Term::Handle</code> give the calculus deep, rebindable effects. An <code>Op</code> is a request with an explicit continuation <code>k</code>; a handler is a natural arrow that returns a record of operation implementations.</p><p>An operation declaration like <code>query : (String, Array) ~[(Op, Query)]~&gt; Value</code> lowers to <code>Term::Op { name: &quot;query&quot;, grade: [Op, Query] }</code>. Installing a handler scopes an arrow under it — <code>Term::Handle</code> — and every <code>Op</code> inside whose grade row includes <code>Query</code> is dispatched to that handler. The handler is an ordinary arrow value, not a special language construct; handler installation is the elimination of the modal grade.</p><h2 id="user-space-algebras" tabindex="-1">User-space algebras <a class="header-anchor" href="#user-space-algebras" aria-label="Permalink to “User-space algebras”">​</a></h2><p>Query/command/event-style algebras are not kernel concepts. They are packages that export grades and operation arrows:</p><ul><li><code>Query</code> — a resumable request/response operation, possibly cacheable.</li><li><code>Command</code> / <code>Mutate</code> — a state-changing request.</li><li><code>Event</code> / <code>Emit</code> — a fire-and-forget notification.</li></ul><p>All of them lower to <code>Term::Op</code> and <code>Term::Handle</code>. The runtime knows only the calculus; it never pattern-matches on the strings &quot;query&quot; or &quot;event&quot;. The algebra and typing live in package code.</p><h2 id="relationship-to-the-surface-language" tabindex="-1">Relationship to the surface language <a class="header-anchor" href="#relationship-to-the-surface-language" aria-label="Permalink to “Relationship to the surface language”">​</a></h2><p>Surface declarations are syntactic sugar for <code>Term::Op</code> and <code>Term::Handle</code> bindings. Records, <code>do</code> blocks, natural arrow assignments, and graded arrows all lower into the same GMSAC terms — the calculus never sees the sugar.</p><h2 id="summary" tabindex="-1">Summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#summary" aria-label="Permalink to “Summary”">​</a></h2><p>GMSAC lets Handl keep a small, reusable kernel — <code>Arr</code>, <code>Op</code>/<code>Handle</code>, and the arrow combinators — while allowing arbitrary user-space effect algebras to be declared in packages. The grade row is the user-defined modality; the kernel only dispatches on it.</p>`,25)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};